  "history": {
    "origins": "Traversone (also Ciapanò, Rovescino) is the reverse game of Tressette. The name Ciapanò is Milanese dialect for \"Don't catch it!\". It was very popular in Lombardy until the 1980s and is still played there; in Central Italy it is known as Traversone. The goal is to avoid taking point cards—whoever reaches the agreed penalty total (often 21 or 31) loses. Play is anticlockwise like most Italian card games.",
    "originCountries": ["Italy"],
    "timeline": ["20th century: Popular in Lombardy; reverse variant of Tressette documented."],
    "evolution": "Popular alternative to standard Tressette across Italy.",
    "cultural": "Common in Italian cafes.",
    "hasPlaceholders": false
  },
